Project IV: Simple Chat Server (chatd)
======================================

Authors
-------
Zeal Shah (NetID: zss37)
Radha Ghate (NetID: rsg169)

Overview
--------
This project implements a simple Internet chat server (`chatd`) using the sockets API and the `poll()` system call to handle multiple clients concurrently. The server allows users to choose a unique screen name, set a status message, send messages to all users (#all) or specific users, and query information.

The server is single-threaded, uses a state machine for parsing, and strictly follows the protocol specified in the assignment, including proper message formatting, validation, and error handling.

Compilation
-----------
To compile the server and test client, simply run:

    make all
    make test_client

This processes the included source files and produces the following executables/scripts:
`chatd`: The main server implementation.
`test_client`: A simple client for testing.
`test_chatd.sh`: A script to run automated tests.

Running the Server
------------------
Start the server by specifying a listening port (e.g., 18088):

    ./chatd 18088

Testing
-------
Run:

    make test

This builds chatd and the test client, starts chatd on port 18088, runs a simple client session, checks NAM registration, WHO #all,
SET status, and WHO for a named user.

Additional manual testing was performed with multiple instances of test/test_client to check private messages, #all broadcasts, duplicate names, unknown recipients, invalid names/statuses/messages, and malformed input handling.

1.  Terminal 1 (Server):
    ./chatd 18088

2.  Terminal 2 (Test Suite):
    ./test_chatd.sh

This runs several test scenarios:
1.  Basic Functionality: Verifies client connection, `NAM` registration, welcome messages, and appearance in `WHO #all`.
2.  Multiple Clients: Connects multiple clients simultaneously to ensure `#all` broadcasts and private messages route correctly.
3.  Status Handling: Uses `SET` to assign statuses, verifies broadcasts on non-empty statuses, and checks `WHO` outputs.
4.  Error Handling & Edge Cases: Checks for handling of duplicate names (ERR 1), unknown recipients (ERR 2), illegal characters (ERR 3), message too long (ERR 4), and connection closures for malformed input (ERR 0).

Manual Testing
You can also connect to the chat server interactively using `test_client`.

test_client (Interactive Client)
Usage: `./test/test_client <host> <port> <name>`
Connects to the server and allows you to manually send protocol commands to interact with other connected users.

    ./test/test_client 127.0.0.1 18088 Bob

Supported Commands:
`MSG <recipient> <message>`: Send a message to a specific user or `#all`.
`WHO <name or #all>`: Query specific user details or everyone online.
`SET <status>`: Set your status message.
`QUIT`: Exit the client.